--- body ---
## Context

The README (235 lines) is doing five jobs at once: pitch, quickstart, design
essay, install, security verification, and contributor/CI policy. The top third
is clean user-facing prose; jargon density and maintainer-facing asides spike
once "design depth" begins.

An Imagine-phase detection pass + a two-Sonnet plan review (user/IA lens +
information-loss/accuracy lens) surfaced that several "moves" in the naive plan
were disguised deletions: the relocation destinations do not already contain the
facts, and several de-jargon substitutions lose precision the PEP and threat
model rely on.

Guiding principle (the thread through every decision below):
**the README points at generated/canonical sources; it does not duplicate them.**

Scope: README.md and CONTRIBUTING.md only. Doc-only. No src/go changes.
Out of scope: enriching command help text (Option B was rejected -- the
generated help is already richer than the README; see "Decisions" item 1).

## Decisions (resolved during Imagine + review)

1. **"Managing dependencies" / "Updating" sections -> trim to a pointer (Option A,
   not B).** `erg close --help` and `erg update --help` are far richer than the
   README's terse restatements and are already generated + drift-checked into
   `docs/erg-manual.md`. The README copies are thin, drift-prone, and one has a
   grammar bug ("will lookup for"). Do NOT relocate prose into `erg-manual.md`:
   it is auto-generated by `make docs` from `erg --help --all` and guarded by
   `tests/test_docs.sh` -- hand edits would be overwritten and fail the drift
   test. Enriching the Go help text (Option B) is unnecessary; the help is
   already excellent.

2. **Source map -> orientation in README, detail in CONTRIBUTING.** Keep the
   README "Specification" block as a 4-pointer orientation (spec / reference impl
   / rationale / contributing). The file-level map (helptext.go drives the
   registry, main.go dispatches, the five-places rule) stays in CONTRIBUTING
   ("Where things live" + "Adding a subcommand"). No broad source map in README.
   Dedup the overlap so the two do not drift.

3. **No `erg help` command-list table in README.** The 16-command list IS
   generated content (`erg --help --all` -> erg-manual.md); copying it into the
   README is the same drift trap, unguarded by any test. Replace with one line:
   "`erg help` lists all commands; full reference in `docs/erg-manual.md`." The
   existing capability teaser in prose stays.

4. **Relocations are not deletions.** For every fact moved out of the README,
   verify the destination contains it (add it if not) before removing it from
   the README.

## Exit criteria

E1 -- Strip internal ticket IDs from Binary policy.
- [ ] Remove "delivered in ticket 0151" (x2) ; keep the reproducibility claims.

E2 -- Binary-policy build/CI mechanics: dedup + relocate, do not delete.
- [ ] The `build/erg`-not-`tickets/erg` rule is already in CONTRIBUTING (L25-26):
      delete the duplicate from README, do not re-add.
- [ ] ADD the `make update-bootstrap-binary` cadence and the "never modified by
      `make test`" invariant to CONTRIBUTING (they are NOT there today).
- [ ] README "Binary policy" keeps only the user claim: source is primary, the
      committed binary is a verifiable cache, why it ships.
- [ ] Fix the now-stale pointer at README L126-127 ("build details are in Binary
      policy below") so it points to CONTRIBUTING.

E3 -- Remove the author aside "I look forward to working with macOS, ARM or
     Windows early adopters."
- [ ] Deleted from README (non-Linux build path at L115 already covers the fact).

E4 -- Reword "have a capable AI review src/go/" to reader-neutral phrasing.
- [ ] De-personalized BUT retains the substance "reviewable source beats an
      opaque blob" and a concrete next action (it has no other home).

E5 -- Shrink the third-transport essay (L81-85) to one sentence + pointer to
     pep-erg-v1.md section 8 (which covers it fully, L176-227).
- [ ] Replacement does NOT harden pep section 8's deliberately open "the door is
      not closed" stance into a closure claim.
- [ ] Rewrite/remove the orphaned "design depth" transition at L57-62.

E6 -- Trim "Verifying the binary" prose; keep all security-load-bearing items.
- [ ] KEEP in README (none are in threat-model.md/red-team-checklist.md):
      self-attestation caveat (L197-199), release-cadence-vs-CI-cadence
      distinction (L204-206), full GPG fingerprint (L216), GPG import commands +
      UID (L211-214, L217).
- [ ] Cut only navigational/threat-model prose that the existing
      docs/threat-model.md link already covers. Keep every runnable command
      (sha256sum, make verify, git verify-tag).

E7 -- Conservative de-jargon. Drop ONLY the "transport #1/#2/#3" numbering;
     keep the "POSIX first, CLI second" section name and the 4-user table.
- [ ] Do NOT swap these (review flagged precision loss): "version-skew"
      (PEP uses it, section 8 L210), "vendored source" (supply-chain meaning),
      "keystone control" (matches threat-model.md), "transport" (defined
      vocabulary cross-referenced to the PEP).

E8 -- Grammar/polish, scoped to two lines.
- [ ] L154 run-on tightened; L158 "will lookup for" -> "looks up ... replaces
      the binary in place."

E9 -- A/B sections land per Decision 1.
- [ ] "Managing dependencies" and "Updating" trimmed to one sentence + pointer to
      `erg <cmd> --help` / docs/erg-manual.md.

Gate
- [ ] `make check` (or `make test`) passes; no new drift failures.
- [ ] No fact removed from the README without a verified home elsewhere.
